+A `return` statement without a value returns `nil` from a partial.
 
 ## Example
 
@@ -62,32 +59,24 @@ Hugo renders:
 
 ## Usage
 
-> [!NOTE]
-> Unlike `return` statements in other languages, Hugo executes the first occurrence of the `return` statement regardless of its position within logical blocks.
-
-A partial that returns a value must contain only one `return` statement, placed at the end of the template.
+The `return` statement may be placed anywhere in a template, including inside `if`, `with`, and `range` blocks. Output rendered before the `return` is discarded when the template is executed as a partial that returns a value; in other templates it is kept.
 
 For example:
 
-```go-html-template {file="layouts/_partials/is-even.html"}
-{{ $result := false }}
+```go-html-template {file="layouts/_partials/parity.html"}
 {{ if math.ModBool . 2 }}
-  {{ $result = "even" }}
-{{ else }}
-  {{ $result = "odd" }}
+  {{ return "even" }}
 {{ end }}
-{{ return $result }}
+{{ return "odd" }}
 ```
 
-> [!NOTE]
-> The construct below is incorrect; it contains more than one `return` statement.
+A `return` in a template invoked with `template` (including `define` and `block`) terminates that template only.
 
-```go-html-template {file="layouts/_partials/do-not-do-this.html"}
-{{ if math.ModBool . 2 }}
-  {{ return "even" }}
-{{ else }}
-  {{ return "odd" }}
-{{ end }}
+In regular templates, use `return` to exit early:
+
+```go-html-template {file="layouts/page.html"}
+{{ with .Params.hidden }}{{ return }}{{ end }}
+<h1>{{ .Title }}</h1>
 ```
 
